{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2181-12-28 22:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee, one of the hospitalists. You’re here because of abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. Can you tell me what’s been going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2181-12-28 22:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes. A few days ago, I started feeling nauseous again. Yesterday it got worse—I couldn’t keep anything down, and I felt dizzy just standing up. I’ve barely peed in over a day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2181-12-28 22:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And your G-tube has been draining more than usual?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2181-12-28 22:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, more output than normal. I called my doctor and tried Gatorade, but it didn’t help. I knew I might need fluids again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2181-12-28 22:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That was the right call. We’ve started IV fluids, and you’ll stay NPO for now—nothing by mouth—to let your gut rest."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2181-12-28 22:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. But the pain is bad. Can I get something for it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2181-12-28 22:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re giving IV Dilaudid for pain and Phenergan for nausea. But we have to be careful—pain meds can sometimes make nausea worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2181-12-28 22:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s happened before. I take morphine at home, but it makes me sicker."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2181-12-28 22:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. That’s part of the cycle we’re trying to break. We’ll monitor your response and adjust."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2181-12-28 23:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your X-ray came back. No sign of a bowel obstruction. That’s good news."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2181-12-28 23:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not a blockage?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2181-12-28 23:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No. It looks like your usual ileus—your gut isn’t moving well due to your fibrosing mesenteritis. We’ll keep supporting you until it recovers."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2181-12-28 23:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That makes sense. It feels like my other flares."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2181-12-29 06:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. We checked your labs. Your kidney function is a little off—creatinine is 1.1—but that’s likely from dehydration. The fluids are helping."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2181-12-29 06:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2181-12-29 06:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not long-term. It’s called prerenal kidney injury—your kidneys are stressed from low volume, but they’ll bounce back with fluids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2181-12-29 06:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that’s a relief."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2181-12-29 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We noticed your blood pressure dropped a bit this morning—systolic in the 70s. You felt dizzy?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2181-12-29 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, a little lightheaded when I sat up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2181-12-29 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We think it’s from the pain medication. We held the IV narcotics for now and increased fluids. You’re stabilizing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2181-12-29 08:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So the morphine might have caused that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2181-12-29 08:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. It can lower blood pressure, especially when you’re dehydrated. We’ll switch back to your oral meds when you’re ready."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2181-12-29 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The chronic pain team reviewed your case. They recommend using IV Toradol in the future instead of narcotics during flares."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2181-12-29 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What’s Toradol?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2181-12-29 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s a non-opioid pain medicine. It works well for abdominal pain and doesn’t worsen nausea like opioids can."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2181-12-29 10:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That sounds better. I’d rather not feel sicker from the pain meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2181-12-29 10:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Agreed. We’ll note that for future admissions. They also suggest Lidoderm patches if your pain is localized."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2181-12-29 12:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs show your hemoglobin dropped from 12.3 to 9.8. But we checked for bleeding and hemolysis—nothing there."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2181-12-29 12:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Am I anemic?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2181-12-29 12:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It looks like a dilutional anemia—your blood got diluted from all the IV fluids. Your counts should normalize as things balance out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2181-12-29 12:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I don’t need a transfusion?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2181-12-29 12:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not at this point. Your body is adjusting. We’ll keep monitoring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2181-12-29 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your INR came back at 2.3—perfect. We’ll keep you on your usual warfarin dose."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2181-12-29 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I’ve had clots before, so I don’t want to mess with that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2181-12-29 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We’re continuing your levothyroxine too—thyroid levels are stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2181-12-29 16:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve been stable for hours. We’re going to try advancing your diet—start with ice chips and see how you do."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2181-12-29 16:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m nervous—last time I tried, I threw up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2181-12-29 16:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll go slow. We’ll give anti-nausea meds ahead of time and watch closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great news—you’ve tolerated liquids and soft foods. Your pain is controlled on your oral morphine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel so much better. Can I go home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. We’re discharging you today. We’re increasing your misoprostol to 200 mcg four times a day to help gut motility."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That was adjusted last time too. I’ll keep track."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Also, continue your home pregabalin and metoclopramide as needed. And follow up with your pain clinic next week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need another hospital stay like this?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:13:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We hope not. But if symptoms return, try oral rehydration first. If you can’t keep fluids down or feel weak, come back."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And no more CT scans unless it’s an emergency?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Correct. The team agrees—your history and X-rays are enough. We’ll avoid unnecessary radiation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel much more confident about managing this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll send all instructions to your primary team. Rest well, and don’t hesitate to reach out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2181-12-30 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks again for explaining everything."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2181-12-28 22:16:00",
    "admission_end": "2181-12-30 20:16:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient, a woman with a history of sclerosing mesenteritis, recurrent partial small bowel obstructions, and chronic GI dysmotility, was admitted for abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and dehydration. Imaging showed no bowel obstruction, consistent with a paralytic ileus. She was managed with NPO status, IV fluids, and IV pain and nausea control. A transient drop in blood pressure was attributed to opioid use and resolved with fluid resuscitation. Her anemia was determined to be dilutional from fluid administration. She transitioned to oral pain medications and tolerated a soft diet. The chronic pain service recommended using IV Toradol in future flares to avoid worsening nausea. She was discharged home with stable vital signs, improved symptoms, and updated medication instructions.",
    "problems": [
      "Sclerosing mesenteritis",
      "Paralytic ileus",
      "Dehydration",
      "Anemia, unspecified",
      "Personal history of venous thrombosis and embolism",
      "Long-term (current) use of anticoagulants",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Unspecified viral hepatitis C without hepatic coma",
      "Chronic hepatitis C without mention of hepatic coma",
      "Gastrostomy status",
      "Sicca syndrome",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified",
      "Asthma, unspecified type, unspecified"
    ]
  }
}
